(Background: Psychosocial stress and functional decline commonly affect lung cancer) Tj
T*
(patients during chemotherapy. Methods: 84 lung cancer patients undergoing active) Tj
T*
(chemotherapy were randomly assigned to a laughter-based exercise intervention \(n=42\) or) Tj
T*
(routine care \(n=40\). The intervention involved structured sessions combining movement and) Tj
T*
(laughter practices. Perceived stress, positive psychological resources, and exercise) Tj
T*
(capacity were measured at baseline and post-intervention. Results: 80 patients \(40 per) Tj
T*
(group\) completed the study. Patients in the laughter-based intervention demonstrated) Tj
T*
(significantly higher positive psychological resources scores compared to routine care) Tj
T*
(\(Cohen's d=0.71; p<0.05\), increased exercise tolerance \(d=0.64; p<0.05\), and notably lower) Tj
T*
(perceived stress ratings \(d=1.09; p<0.05\). Within-group improvements in stress occurred in) Tj
T*
(both arms, but between-group differences favored the intervention. Conclusions: Laughter-) Tj
T*
(based aerobic activities appear effective in reducing stress perception and enhancing) Tj
T*
(exercise tolerance in chemotherapy patients, suggesting potential adjunctive value for) Tj
T*
